---
description: APPLY E2E smoke test best practices WHEN testing UI applications. Covers general guidance for UI smoke checks, browser verification, console/network error detection, and user flow testing. Playwright suggested but not mandatory.
globs: apps/frontend/**, **/*.e2e.*, **/*.spec.ts, **/*.test.ts
alwaysApply: false
---

Smoke Test Strategy:
- Test critical user flows only
- Keep smoke tests fast and focused
- Test happy paths primarily
- Verify core functionality works
- Avoid testing implementation details
- Run smoke tests in CI pipeline

Browser Verification:
- Always verify UI changes in actual browser
- Check browser console for errors
- Check network tab for failed requests
- Test in multiple browsers if needed
- Verify responsive behavior
- Test with real user interactions
- Never rely solely on unit tests for UI

Console Error Detection:
- Check for JavaScript errors in console
- Check for React warnings (if React)
- Check for network request failures
- Verify no unhandled promise rejections
- Check for memory leaks warnings
- Monitor console during test execution

Network Error Detection:
- Verify all API calls succeed
- Check for 4xx/5xx status codes
- Verify CORS issues if applicable
- Check for timeout errors
- Verify authentication flows
- Check for rate limiting issues

User Flow Testing:
- Test complete user journeys
- Test critical paths end-to-end
- Verify state persistence
- Test error recovery flows
- Test loading states
- Test error states
- Verify navigation works

Playwright (Recommended):
- Use Playwright for E2E testing
- Write tests in TypeScript
- Use page object pattern
- Keep tests independent
- Use fixtures for setup
- Test in headless mode in CI
- Capture screenshots on failure

Alternative Tools:
- Use Puppeteer if Playwright unavailable
- Use Cypress for specific use cases
- Use Selenium as last resort
- Choose tool based on project needs

Test Organization:
- Group related tests using describe
- Use descriptive test names
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Keep tests focused and small
- Avoid test interdependencies
- Clean up test data after tests

CI Integration:
- Run smoke tests in CI pipeline
- Fail build on smoke test failures
- Run tests in headless mode
- Capture screenshots on failure
- Report test results clearly
- Use appropriate timeouts
